\\mathcal{L}.description:
The Lagrangian of a physical system, typically defined as the difference between its kinetic energy and potential energy (
\( \mathcal{L} = T - V \n\)
). It is a scalar function that characterizes the dynamics of the system.

temporal-invariance-law-16c94ce1.symmetry_origin:
This law is a direct consequence of the principle of translational symmetry in time. According to Noether's theorem, for every continuous symmetry of a physical system, there is a corresponding conserved quantity. Temporal invariance (i.e., the laws of physics are the same today as they were yesterday and will be tomorrow) is a fundamental symmetry of nature. The continuous symmetry of time translation leads directly to the conservation of energy. If the Lagrangian does not explicitly depend on time (
\( \frac{\partial \mathcal{L}}{\partial t} = 0 \n\)
), then the Hamiltonian, which for conservative systems is equal to the total energy, is conserved.
